{"timestamp_edt": "2026-09-16 21:53:48 EDT", "action_type": "query", "duration_seconds": 8.042783, "file_name": "", "query_text": "What happened to George Perkins when he ran away from his master?", "steps_taken": [{"step": "embed query", "seconds": 3.000878}, {"step": "keyword extraction", "seconds": 0.000506}, {"step": "vector retrieval", "seconds": 0.443415}, {"step": "context assembly", "seconds": 4e-06}, {"step": "llm answer generation", "seconds": 4.596224}], "tools_called": ["RetrievalService.search_similar_chunks", "RetrievalService.search_ancestry_data"], "api_calls_made": ["embedding (ollama)", "llm (deepseek)"], "final_response": "When George Perkins ran away from his master — once in August 1751 and again in November 1751 — he was captured and punished both times. The Accomack County, Virginia court records describe George Perkins, “a Servant belonging to Andrew Gilchrist, Administrator of James Gibson dec’d,” as being punished for “running away from his said master” [footnote 36][footnote 37].", "response_time_seconds": 4.596224, "input_tokens": 4862, "output_tokens": 274, "input_cache_hit_tokens": 128, "input_cache_miss_tokens": 4734, "estimated_cost_usd": 0.00734, "pricing_rate": "peak", "reasoning_mode": "low"}
{"timestamp_edt": "2026-09-16 21:55:47 EDT", "action_type": "query", "duration_seconds": 5.749967, "file_name": "", "query_text": "What is the Redbone community and how are the Perkins connected to it?", "steps_taken": [{"step": "embed query", "seconds": 2.119679}, {"step": "keyword extraction", "seconds": 0.000413}, {"step": "vector retrieval", "seconds": 0.126712}, {"step": "context assembly", "seconds": 2e-06}, {"step": "llm answer generation", "seconds": 3.502349}], "tools_called": ["RetrievalService.search_similar_chunks", "RetrievalService.search_ancestry_data"], "api_calls_made": ["embedding (ollama)", "llm (deepseek)"], "final_response": "The Redbone community is described in the context as a “mixed-race” community located in western Louisiana and Texas [Document 1]. The Perkins connection is direct: Joshua Perkins’ sons Isaac and Joshua (brothers of George) “went to western Louisiana and Texas and were among the founding families of the mixed-race ‘Redbone’ community there” [Document 1]. Supporting sources for the Redbones are cited in footnote 7, including Don C. Marler’s *Redbones of Louisiana* and Patricia Ann Waak’s *My Bones Are Red* [footnote 7].", "response_time_seconds": 3.502349, "input_tokens": 980, "output_tokens": 259, "input_cache_hit_tokens": 128, "input_cache_miss_tokens": 852, "estimated_cost_usd": 0.002156, "pricing_rate": "peak", "reasoning_mode": "low"}
